  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nModels are in good agreement that a Pacific low will move to the \nsouth-central coast of California by early Monday and then move \neastward onshore throughout the day. If it weren't for the lack of \nmoisture on Monday it would be a decent set up for a transitional \nsevere weather event as there will be potential for some \nfrontogenesis with upper-level jet support and higher shear. \nHowever, while PWATs will increase on Monday, they look to remain \naround or slightly below 1\" (0.6-1.0\"). \n\nThen on Tuesday the low will slowly weaken as it moves NE into NV/UT \nbefore getting swept up into the large scale flow on Wednesday as it \nmoves into the northern Plains. At the same time a weak ridge will \nbe in place over TX and will start to strengthen on Wednesday. This \nwill result in southwesterly flow over our area. With the moisture \nreturn and some upper-level forcing from the aforementioned low, \nrain chances will return to the higher terrain areas in northern \nand eastern Arizona. Weak large scale troughing will remain \nacross the eastern Pacific/West Coast through the end of next week\nand will be battling the ridge of high pressure over TX. By the \nend of the workweek the ridge will try to retrograde into NM/AZ. \nAdditionally ensemble members bring a surge of moisture into our \narea from the SE pushing PWATs back into a 1.0-1.3\" range. As a \nresult NBM/WPC PoPs for the end of next week have 30-50% chance \nof showers and storms across the higher terrain in southern Gila \nCounty and around 10-20% across the lower deserts of south-central\nAZ.\n\nTemperatures next week will remain largely unchanged and be in the \nupper 90s to low 100s across the lower deserts and in the upper 80s \nto mid 90s across the higher terrain. Morning lows will generally be \nin the 70s to to low 80s across the lower deserts and in the 60s \nacross the higher terrain areas. Morning lows do look to start to \ncreep up by the end of next week (more morning lows in the low 80s \nacross the lower deserts). As a result widespread Minor HeatRisk \nwill continue through the beginning of next week with Moderate \nHeatRisk returning to the lower deserts for the latter half of next \nweek as the morning lows creep up and daily normals decrease.",
